  <artifactId>s22-operability-deadletters-retention</artifactId>
  <packaging>pom</packaging>
  <name>s22-operability-deadletters-retention</name>
  <description>S22: what a message flow needs before it can be operated — dead letters, replay, retention, and the guards that speak up at startup.</description>

  <!--
    Two modules, because the two halves of "a message went nowhere" are owned by different services
    and neither can see the other's evidence. The publisher's give-up ends in a table it owns
    (aipersimmon_dead_letter) and is replayed through an endpoint it owns. The consumer's give-up
    ends on a topic (<topic>.DLT) that must have been provisioned before the first bad record, and
    nothing the publisher does can rescue it. A sample with one module would have to pick a side and
    would then be silent about the half where most production incidents actually live.

    No third test module (S10 needed one, S12 did not): every claim here is asserted from inside one
    service, against its own tables or its own broker, so the tests belong with the code they judge.
  -->
  <modules>
    <module>ordering-service</module>
    <module>inventory-service</module>
  </modules>
</project>
